m the initial vertex is bounded by the distance between the two vertices plus one. This leads to questioning the maximum possible distance.\n\nA long path between two vertices consumes Roman weight, bounding the \n<truncated 1053 bytes>\n\\ge 2$, then $w \\ge 4$.\n\nSince if $\\text{radius}(G) \\le 3$ then $\\text{radius}(G) \\le w-1$ when $w \\ge 4$, this inequality holds automatically. This logic is restated for greater clarity and confirmation of its implications.\n\n\n**Analyzing Radius Bound Failures**\n\nThe inequality $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le w$ holds trivially if $\\text{radius}(G) \\le 3$. The case where $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 4$ is now under consideration. A path graph with radius 4 is identified as an example.\n\nThe possibility of $\\text{romanWeight}(f) = 4$ when $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 4$ is then explored and proven impossible. If the weight is 4, then k=2, and all vertices are in $N[u_1] \\cup N[u_2]$.\n\nA contradiction is reached because if $V = N[u_1] \\cup N[u_2]$, it was previously shown that the radius is at most 2. This implies no graph with $\\text{radius} \\ge 4$ has a Roman dominating function of weight 4.\n\n\n**Testing Radius-Weight Relationship**\n\nExamined the case where $\\text{romanWeight}(f) = 5$ to check if $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 4$ could be violated. Found $\\text{radius}(G) = 4$ still satisfies the constraint $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le \\text{romanWeight}(f)$.\n\nAttempted to find a graph where $\\text{romanWeight}(f) = 5$ and $\\text{radius}(G) \\ge 5$ to disprove $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le \\text{romanWeight}(f)$. Constructed a graph comprised of two stars with one additional vertex.\n\nThe combined structure cannot have a radius greater than 3, not 4 or 5. This strengthens the observation that $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le \\text{romanWeight}(f)$ is always true.\n\n\n**Revisiting Previous Proof Techniques**\n\nRecalling the previous successful proof strategy, focus on relating graph cardinality to the cardinality of a ball